<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    Pulse — notification small icon
    ===
    Rules this file follows and WHY (they are not obvious):

    1. MONOCHROME SILHOUETTE ONLY.
       Android does NOT render notification small icons as drawn.
       It extracts the alpha channel, throws away RGB, and re-tints
       the shape using "default_notification_color" (pulse_accent)
       or the system default. Any icon with more than one color,
       gradients, or fine internal detail collapses into an
       unrecognizable blob in the status bar. So this file has ONE
       path, ONE fill color (white), and a single clear silhouette.

    2. 24dp × 24dp VIEWPORT, FILLED TO EDGES.
       The system scales the small icon to ~24dp regardless of the
       declared width/height, so designing on a 24dp grid with the
       shape touching (2,2)–(22,22) is the standard approach. Icons
       designed on a 48dp grid with generous margins appear tiny in
       the status bar.

    3. WHITE (#FFFFFFFF) FILL, TRANSPARENT BACKGROUND.
       The fill color is irrelevant to what the user sees (Android
       tints it), but using white matches Material Design's
       notification icon guidelines and avoids "dark mode makes the
       icon disappear" complaints when the icon is shown on a
       preview or in-app test render.

    4. NO android:tint="..." ON THE VECTOR.
       Adding a tint would fight the system's automatic tint. Let
       the system tint it. If a future non-notification use of this
       drawable needs a tint, use a separate ImageView with
       app:tint instead of changing this file.

    5. VECTOR, NOT BITMAP.
       Vectors render pixel-perfect at every density. A bitmap
       would require one PNG per density bucket (mdpi/hdpi/xhdpi/
       xxhdpi/xxxhdpi) — five files — and still look softer than
       the vector at high DPI. On Termux with no image editor,
       a vector is also the only practical option.

    6. SHAPE = ROUNDED CHAT BUBBLE.
       The universally recognized notification metaphor for
       "message received". Alternatives considered:
         - Stylized "P" letterform: loses legibility below 24dp.
         - Waveform/pulse glyph: too abstract, ambiguous intent.
         - Bubble with dots inside: too much detail at 24dp.
       A clean bubble reads instantly at any size, in any color.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Single filled path: rounded chat bubble with a pointed tail
        at the bottom-left.

        Path breakdown (for future reference if this ever needs
        editing — the coordinates are non-obvious from the shorthand):
          M 20,2              → start at top-right
          H 4                 → horizontal line to top-left
          c -1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2   → curve into top-left corner, to (2,4)
          v 18                → line down left edge to (2,22)
          l 4,-4              → diagonal up-right to (6,18)  [tail]
          h 14                → horizontal right to (20,18)
          c 1.1,0 2,-0.9 2,-2 → curve into bottom-right corner, to (22,16)
          V 4                 → line up right edge to (22,4)
          c 0,-1.1 -0.9,-2 -2,-2  → curve into top-right corner, back to (20,2)
          z                   → close

        Net shape: a rounded rectangle spanning (2,4)–(22,16) with
        a triangle tail descending to (2,22).
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M20,2H4c-1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2v18l4,-4h14c1.1,0 2,-0.9 2,-2V4c0,-1.1 -0.9,-2 -2,-2z" />

</vector>
